{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "91",
    "case_summary": "An 82-year-old woman was admitted to Broca University Hospital following a fall. Her family reported that she had been experiencing cognitive impairment for several months. She was being treated for hypertension with Candesartan and her type 2 diabetes was managed through diet. Upon admission, physical examination revealed bilateral, symmetrical parkinsonism with reduced arm swing during gait. She exhibited bilateral bradykinesia, limb rigidity, and hypomimia. Her voice was monotonous and hypophonic. There was no tremor or orthostatic hypotension. She scored 18/30 on the Mini-Mental Status Examination (MMSE) and performed poorly on the Cognitive Efficiency Profile, indicating significant dysexecutive syndrome and memory impairment. Deficits were also noted in visuospatial ability, while naming ability was preserved. Basic biological screening tests, including blood cell count, blood chemistry, C-reactive protein, thyroid stimulating hormone, vitamin B12, and folic acid, were normal. A non-injected computed tomography brain scan was also normal. An electroencephalogram showed slow waves, particularly in the left temporal lobe. Following her evaluation, her akinesia and rigidity worsened, and a frontal syndrome developed. L-dopa and Galantamine treatments were introduced without any noted improvement. Two months after hospital admission, her MMSE score had declined to 5/30.\n\nCerebral magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with gadolinium injection highlighted a diffuse tumor-related infiltration involving both lobes, the putamen, the pallidum, the substantia nigra and the brainstem.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Cognitive-Motor Deficit with Bilateral Parkinsonian Features and Frontal Syndrome",
